# serializer version: 1
# name: TestPromptSnapshots.test_categorize_batch_prompt
  '''
  
  You are a culinary classifier. The user has just added these tags to their taxonomy:
  - Cuisine: "American", "British", "French", "Italian"
  - Meal Type: "Breakfast", "Dessert", "Dinner", "Bread"
  
  For EACH recipe below, list which of the tags above it belongs under. Rules:
  - Use ONLY tags from the list above, spelled exactly. Never invent a tag.
  - Return an empty list when none fits.
  - Return one result per recipe id, in any order.
  
  TAGGING RULES:
  - A tag must be true of the dish as a whole, not of one ingredient in it or one step in making it.
  - The test for every tag: would a cook browsing that tag expect to find this recipe there? If not, leave it off.
    * An ingredient that binds, enriches, seasons, garnishes, or makes the stock or sauce does not make
      the dish about that ingredient: eggs in a dough, chicken stock in a soup, butter in a sauce.
    * A step on the way does not name how the dish is cooked: browning meat before braising it,
      boiling pasta before baking it.
    * The same test leaves off a tag the whole dish fails: a soup made with chicken stock is not meat-free.
  - Usually one tag per axis. Choose a second only when the dish belongs equally under both.
  - An axis with no good answer stays empty, and an empty axis is one of the commonest right
    answers. Here is the mistake to watch for in yourself: an axis looks unanswered, so you reach
    for the nearest thing the recipe mentions and tag that. If the only candidate you can find on
    an axis is something the dish merely contains, or merely does on the way, then there is no
    answer on that axis and you return [] for it. Leaving it empty is the right answer, not a
    failure to find one.
  - A tag listed as "A" (under "B") is a kind of B. Choose the most specific tag that fits,
    never together with a tag it is under.
  
  RECIPES:
  RECIPE ID: r1
  TITLE: Lasagne
  INGREDIENTS:
    - pasta
  DIRECTIONS:
    1. Bake.
  
  '''
# ---
# name: TestPromptSnapshots.test_categorize_batch_prompt_for_one_axis
  '''
  
  You are a culinary classifier. The user has just added these tags to their taxonomy:
  - Cuisine: "American", "British", "French", "Italian"
  
  For EACH recipe below, list which of the tags above it belongs under. Rules:
  - Use ONLY tags from the list above, spelled exactly. Never invent a tag.
  - Return an empty list when none fits.
  - Return one result per recipe id, in any order.
  
  TAGGING RULES:
  - A tag must be true of the dish as a whole, not of one ingredient in it or one step in making it.
  - The test for every tag: would a cook browsing that tag expect to find this recipe there? If not, leave it off.
    * An ingredient that binds, enriches, seasons, garnishes, or makes the stock or sauce does not make
      the dish about that ingredient: eggs in a dough, chicken stock in a soup, butter in a sauce.
    * A step on the way does not name how the dish is cooked: browning meat before braising it,
      boiling pasta before baking it.
    * The same test leaves off a tag the whole dish fails: a soup made with chicken stock is not meat-free.
  - Usually one tag per axis. Choose a second only when the dish belongs equally under both.
  - An axis with no good answer stays empty, and an empty axis is one of the commonest right
    answers. Here is the mistake to watch for in yourself: an axis looks unanswered, so you reach
    for the nearest thing the recipe mentions and tag that. If the only candidate you can find on
    an axis is something the dish merely contains, or merely does on the way, then there is no
    answer on that axis and you return [] for it. Leaving it empty is the right answer, not a
    failure to find one.
  - A tag listed as "A" (under "B") is a kind of B. Choose the most specific tag that fits,
    never together with a tag it is under.
  
  RECIPES:
  RECIPE ID: recipe-1
  TITLE: Lasagne
  INGREDIENTS:
    - pasta
  DIRECTIONS:
    1. Bake.
  
  '''
# ---
# name: TestPromptSnapshots.test_classify_prompt
  '''
  You are a grocery shopping classifier. For each ingredient below, say what a shopper buys for it.
  
  Rules:
  - "food" names what a shopper buys, singular and lower case ("egg", not "Eggs").
  - Reuse a food from KNOWN FOODS when the ingredient is the same thing, spelled exactly as the known food is.
  - Keep a variety only when it changes what is bought: "red onion" stays "red onion"; "diced onion" is "onion"; "free-range eggs" is "egg".
  - Never equate functionally distinct products, even when a KNOWN FOOD is close: baking soda is not baking powder, sweetened condensed milk is not evaporated milk, table salt is not flaky finishing salt.
  - "aisle" is the store aisle the food is found in, one of: produce, meat_fish, dairy_eggs, bakery, dry_goods, spices, condiments, tins_jars, frozen, drinks, household, other.
  - "pantry" is true for what a typical home kitchen keeps in stock (salt, oil, flour, dried spices).
  - "count" is how many whole units a shopper buys for this ingredient (2.2 onion, 0.3 head garlic, 1 tin). When "count" is set, "count_unit" names what is counted: the food itself, singular ("onion", "egg"), or how it is bought ("tin", "head", "bunch") - never a cooking measure ("cup", "tbsp"). Use null for both "count" and "count_unit" when the food is bought by weight or volume (flour, milk, mince).
  - Answer every ingredient by its "key", each exactly once.
  
  KNOWN FOODS:
  ["flour"]
  
  INGREDIENTS:
  {"key": "r1:i1", "text": "2 cups plain flour", "name": "plain flour", "amount": 2.0, "unit": "cup"}
  {"key": "r1:i2", "text": "salt to taste", "name": "salt", "amount": null, "unit": null}
  
  '''
# ---
# name: TestPromptSnapshots.test_direction_mentions_prompt
  '''
  
  You are a culinary data refiner. For the recipe below, find every mention of an ingredient in the
  directions and say how much of that ingredient each mention uses.
  
  RULES:
     - Report the mentions of each step in the order they appear in it.
     - "quote" is the mention's words, copied character for character from that step.
     - "context" is the quote with about five words around it, copied character for character from
       the step, so two mentions with the same words can be told apart.
     - "ingredient_id" is the id of the ingredient line the words refer to.
     - A heading step ("**Phase 1**") has no mentions.
     - A heading line is never a mention: no "ingredient_id" may name a line marked (heading).
     - Say how much of the ingredient each mention uses, as "use":
         * all  - the whole amount the ingredient line gives goes in here, at once. Only the FIRST
           time it does: later mentions of the same food ("drain the rice", "until the rice is
           done", "the butter mixture") are none.
         * an amount - the direction itself writes a quantity for this mention. The words are ONLY
           that quantity as written ("1/2 cup (60 g)", "2", "two tablespoons"), never the
           ingredient's name ("Add 1 cup peas": the words are "1 cup", not "1 cup peas"), and the
           use is the quantity as a decimal number and a unit: "0.5 cup", "60 g", "2" for a count.
           If the direction names the ingredient without writing a quantity beside it, the use is
           all, rest or none - never an amount, even when you know the line's amount.
         * rest - what is left after amounts the directions stated earlier: "the remaining flour",
           "the rest of the sugar". The words are the ingredient's name.
         * none - the ingredient is named but no amount follows from the text: "add more flour
           until sticky", "a bit more", "season with salt", "salt to taste", "Salt it", a share
           ("half the cider", "a third of the flour"), a rate ("1 teaspoon at a time", "2 per
           ball"), or a later mention of food already added.
     - When unsure, say "none". A missing number is safe; a wrong one is not.
     - Wrap only the ingredient's words, never "the", "your" or "of": "the flour" -> the words are "flour".
     - Examples, as step text -> quote, context, use:
         "Whisk together the flour and salt" -> "flour", "Whisk together the flour and", all;
                                                "salt", "the flour and salt", all
         "Place the butter and sugar in a bowl" -> "butter", "Place the butter and sugar", all;
                                                   "sugar", "butter and sugar in a bowl", all
         "Heat 1 tablespoon of the oil" -> "1 tablespoon", "Heat 1 tablespoon of the oil", "1 tablespoon"
         "Add about 1/2 cup (60 g) flour; add more flour until sticky"
             -> "1/2 cup (60 g)", "Add about 1/2 cup (60 g) flour", "0.5 cup";
                "flour", "add more flour until sticky", none
         "Add the remaining flour" -> "flour", "Add the remaining flour", rest
  
  INGREDIENTS:
    ing_01: 2 cups/250g plain flour
    ing_02: 1 cup sugar
  
  DIRECTIONS:
    1. Mix everything.
    2. Bake until done.
  
  '''
# ---
# name: TestPromptSnapshots.test_extract_prompt[extract]
  '''
  
  You are a culinary data extractor. Review the following text from an EPUB recipe book.
  Extract ALL distinct recipes found in the text.
  
  Rules:
  - If you see a [HERO IMAGE: filename.jpg] marker, ALWAYS use that filename as
    photo_filename — it is the confirmed finished-dish photo for this recipe.
  - Otherwise, if you see [IMAGE: filename.jpg] markers, assign exactly ONE filename
    to photo_filename: the image most likely to be the hero/finished-dish photo.
    The hero image can appear in any of these positions:
      a) immediately BEFORE the recipe title or ingredient list, OR
      b) immediately AFTER the last ingredient and BEFORE the first method step.
    Both positions are common depending on the book's layout.
    IGNORE images that appear embedded WITHIN the numbered method steps — those are
    instructional process shots, not the finished dish.
    If there is only one [IMAGE:] marker in the recipe, use it unless it is clearly
    mid-method (e.g. appears after "Step 2" or "Step 3" text).
    If no hero image is identifiable, leave photo_filename null.
  - Copy every ingredient line exactly as the text writes it: the same numbers, the same units,
    every measure the line gives, in the same order. Never convert, round, drop or add a measure.
    The one change allowed: write unicode fractions (½, ¼, ¾, etc.) as plain text (1/2, 1/4, 3/4, etc.).
  - If a recipe uses multiple phases, stages, or days (e.g. "PHASE 1 / PHASE 2",
    "Day 1 / Day 2", "Soaker / Final Dough"), preserve ALL phases in full.
    Insert the phase label as a bold heading entry using Markdown bold syntax, e.g.:
      ingredients: ["**Phase 1**", "28g whole wheat flour", "28g pineapple juice",
                    "**Phase 2**", "56g whole wheat flour", "56g water"]
      directions:  ["**Phase 1**", "Mix flour and juice.", "**Phase 2**", "Add remaining flour."]
    The bold label must be its own separate list item, followed by that phase's
    ingredients or steps as normal list items.
    Do NOT flatten, merge, or skip any phase — the reader must follow them in order.
  - If a field is entirely absent from the text, leave it null.
  - stated_source is the publication or book as the text names itself; byline is the author's
    name if one is printed. Never the name of a model or a tool. Leave both null if the text
    does not say.
  - total_time: the recipe's stated total or overall time, only when the text states one.
    Never add prep and cook together.
  - description: the headnote or introduction printed with the recipe, in its own words, at
    most one paragraph. null when there is none.
  - nutritional_info: the recipe's nutrition statement, verbatim, as one line. null when the
    text carries none.
  - Do not invent or infer values that are not present in the text.
  
  Text chunk:
  PLACEHOLDER CHUNK BODY — fixed text so the snapshot only moves when the template does.
  
  '''
# ---
# name: TestPromptSnapshots.test_plain_text_prompt
  '''
  
  You are a culinary data extractor. The following text is a recipe. Extract it.
  
  Rules:
  - Extract the recipe title, servings, prep time, cook time, ingredients, and directions.
  - Ingredients: one item per list entry, copied exactly as the text writes it: the same numbers,
    the same units, every measure the line gives, in the same order. Never convert, round, drop or
    add a measure. The one change allowed: write unicode fractions (½, ¼, ¾) as plain text (1/2, 1/4, 3/4).
  - Directions: one step per list entry.
  - If a field is absent from the text, leave it null.
  - stated_source is the publication or book as the text names itself; byline is the author's
    name if one is printed. Never the name of a model or a tool. Leave both null if the text
    does not say.
  - total_time: the recipe's stated total or overall time, only when the text states one.
    Never add prep and cook together.
  - description: the headnote or introduction printed with the recipe, in its own words, at
    most one paragraph. null when there is none.
  - nutritional_info: the recipe's nutrition statement, verbatim, as one line. null when the
    text carries none.
  - Do not invent or infer values not present in the text.
  - photo_filename: always null (no images in plain text).
  
  Text:
  PLACEHOLDER CHUNK BODY — fixed text so the snapshot only moves when the template does.
  
  '''
# ---
# name: TestPromptSnapshots.test_refine_prompt
  '''
  
  You are a culinary data refiner. Transform this raw recipe into the structured Cayenne format.
  
  RULES:
  1. STRUCTURED INGREDIENTS:
     - Assign each entry a unique ID (ing_01, ing_02, etc.).
     - Extract numeric "amount", "unit" (null if unitless), and "name".
     - "fallback_string" is the original full line, unchanged.
     - Every line of the ingredients list gets exactly one entry, in the order the lines appear,
       and "line_index" is that line's 0-based position in the RAW RECIPE ingredients list.
     - "kind": "heading" for a line that names a part of the recipe and lists nothing to buy:
       "For the sauce:", "PUDDING", "To serve:", "Garnish", a bold phase such as "**Phase 1**".
       "kind": "ingredient" for every other line, including a food with no amount
       ("Butter for greasing", "Salt to taste", "To serve: crusty bread").
     - A heading has "name" set to its words without emphasis or a trailing colon, "fallback_string"
       the line unchanged, and "amount", "unit", "converted_amount", "converted_unit", "state" null.
       Number headings in the same ing_01, ing_02 sequence as ingredients.
     - STATE: for every ingredient with an amount and a volume or weight unit, say whether it is
       "liquid" - pourable as the recipe uses it: water, milk, cream, stock, oil, wine, juice,
       vinegar, honey, syrup, melted butter - or "solid" - everything else, including flour,
       sugar, salt, cold butter, chopped vegetables and meat. Put it in "state". Leave "state"
       null when the line has no amount or its unit is neither a volume nor a weight.
       Use the state when you compute the conversion below.
     - DUAL MEASURES: when a line states two measures ("1 cup (240 g) flour", "250g/2 cups flour",
       "1 ounce (about 1/3 packed cup) grated pecorino"), the FIRST measure the writer gives is
       "amount" and "unit". When the second is the other kind (a weight beside a volume, or a volume
       beside a weight), put it in "converted_amount" and "converted_unit" exactly as the line writes
       it and set "is_ai_converted" to false. When both are the same kind ("1 pound (450g)"), the
       second is not structured: apply the CONVERSION rule below.
     - CONVERSION: for a line with one measure, give the equivalent in the OTHER measure, in grams or
       millilitres only: a weight for a volume ("1 cup" flour -> 120, "g"), a volume for a weight
       ("150 g" flour -> 300, "ml"). Never answer in cups, spoons, ounces or pints. Put it in
       "converted_amount" and "converted_unit" and set "is_ai_converted" to true. Leave
       "converted_amount" and "converted_unit" null and "is_ai_converted" false when the line
       states no amount ("salt to taste") or its unit is neither a volume nor a weight ("3 eggs",
       "a pinch").
       Sizes for older British measures: gill = 142 ml (a US gill = 118 ml); teacup = 142 ml; breakfast cup = 227 ml; dessertspoon = 10 ml; stone = 14 lb; dram = 1/16 oz (a weight).
     - Never change a number or a unit the line writes: "amount" and "unit" are the line's own.
     - amount: the numeric quantity. Use null - never 0 - when the source states no
       amount ("salt to taste", "a pinch of nutmeg"). A zero would be read as a real
       measurement of nothing.
  
  2. TOKENIZED DIRECTIONS:
     - Rewrite each direction with Fat Tokens: {{ingredient_id|words|use}}. Copy the direction
       exactly; replacing every token with its words must give back the direction as written.
     - Say how much of the ingredient each mention uses, as "use":
         * all  - the whole amount the ingredient line gives goes in here, at once. Only the FIRST
           time it does: later mentions of the same food ("drain the rice", "until the rice is
           done", "the butter mixture") are none.
         * an amount - the direction itself writes a quantity for this mention. The words are ONLY
           that quantity as written ("1/2 cup (60 g)", "2", "two tablespoons"), never the
           ingredient's name ("Add 1 cup peas": the words are "1 cup", not "1 cup peas"), and the
           use is the quantity as a decimal number and a unit: "0.5 cup", "60 g", "2" for a count.
           If the direction names the ingredient without writing a quantity beside it, the use is
           all, rest or none - never an amount, even when you know the line's amount.
         * rest - what is left after amounts the directions stated earlier: "the remaining flour",
           "the rest of the sugar". The words are the ingredient's name.
         * none - the ingredient is named but no amount follows from the text: "add more flour
           until sticky", "a bit more", "season with salt", "salt to taste", "Salt it", a share
           ("half the cider", "a third of the flour"), a rate ("1 teaspoon at a time", "2 per
           ball"), or a later mention of food already added.
     - When unsure, say "none". A missing number is safe; a wrong one is not.
     - Wrap only the ingredient's words, never "the", "your" or "of": "the flour" -> the words are "flour".
     - Examples:
         "Whisk together the flour and salt" -> "Whisk together the {{ing_01|flour|all}} and {{ing_03|salt|all}}"
         "Add about 1/2 cup (60 g) flour" -> "Add about {{ing_02|1/2 cup (60 g)|0.5 cup}} flour"
         "Add the remaining flour" -> "Add the remaining {{ing_02|flour|rest}}"
         "Add more flour until sticky" -> "Add more {{ing_02|flour|none}} until sticky"
     - TEMPERATURES: wrap every temperature a direction states - an oven, a grill, frying oil, sugar,
       water, proving, a core temperature ("until the centre reads 63°C"), a fridge or a freezer - in a
       temperature token {{temp:VALUE|words}}. The words are the direction's own, copied exactly,
       and the whole run when it gives one setting several ways: "180°C/160°C fan/gas mark 4" is ONE
       token. VALUE is what the writer meant, in the writer's own unit:
         * the figure: a number and C or F ("180C", "350F"), or a Gas Mark ("gas4", "gas0.5", "gas0.25");
         * then ",oven" when it is an oven setting (bake, roast, preheat, a described oven);
         * then ",fan=160C" (or F) when the writer also gives the fan-oven figure;
         * "fan=160C" alone when the writer gives only a fan-oven figure.
         * ",core" (and nothing else after the figure) when it is the food's internal temperature,
           the doneness check: "until the centre reads 200°F", "an internal temperature of 93°C".
       A number with no unit ("bake at 180") or a described oven: decide from the recipe and its
       SOURCE SYSTEM; a described oven is slow 150C, moderate 180C, hot 220C, very hot 230C, each
       with ",oven". Never wrap a range ("170-180°C") or words with no figure ("room temperature",
       "warm", "lukewarm").
     - Examples:
         "Preheat the oven to 350°F" -> "Preheat the oven to {{temp:350F,oven|350°F}}"
         "Bake at 180°C/160°C fan/gas mark 4" -> "Bake at {{temp:180C,oven,fan=160C|180°C/160°C fan/gas mark 4}}"
         "Heat the oil to 180C" -> "Heat the oil to {{temp:180C|180C}}"
         "Bake in a moderate oven" -> "Bake in a {{temp:180C,oven|moderate oven}}"
         "Bake until it reads 93°C in the centre" -> "Bake until it reads {{temp:93C,core|93°C}} in the centre"
  
  3. PHASES:
     - If the raw recipe groups its ingredients or directions into phases,
       stages, or days (a bold heading entry such as "**Phase 1**", "**Day 1**",
       "**Soaker**"), keep every one of those headings as its own entry, in
       place, in both lists.
     - A heading is not an ingredient: in the ingredients list it is an entry with "kind": "heading"
       (rule 1); in the directions it is a step whose text is the heading, with no fat tokens.
       Never put a heading's id in a fat token.
     - Do NOT flatten, merge, renumber, or drop a phase. The reader must still
       be able to tell where one session ends and the next begins.
  
  4. SOURCE SYSTEM:
     - "source_uom_system_detected": the measuring system the writer used, exactly one of "US",
       "UK", "EU", "AU", "Imperial", judged only from evidence in the RAW RECIPE or the SOURCE HOST:
         * a size the recipe states for a unit: "1 cup (240 ml)" or "(237 ml)" is US; "1 cup (250 ml)"
           is a metric cup (UK, EU or AU); "1 tbsp (20 ml)" is AU; "1 pint (568 ml)" or a 20 fl oz
           pint is Imperial;
         * the pre-metric British measures: "breakfast cup", "teacup" or "stone" as a measure, or "gill" when nothing points to the US, is Imperial; "dessertspoon" alone is not evidence (modern UK and Australian recipes still use it);
         * a statement about the measures ("Australian standard measures", "US cup measures");
         * the SOURCE HOST: .co.uk is UK, .com.au is AU, .co.nz is UK (New Zealand's measures are UK
           Metric's);
         * spelling and ingredient names only one country uses ("caster sugar", "plain flour" and
           "double cream" are UK; "all-purpose flour" and "heavy cream" are US).
       Grams printed beside cups are not evidence: American writers print them too.
     - "source_uom_system_evidence": the words you relied on, quoted exactly from the RAW RECIPE, or
       the SOURCE HOST exactly as given. One short quote.
     - Leave both null when nothing above applies.
  
  5. TINS AND BAKING:
     - "pans": every baking tin, pan, tray or dish the recipe names with a size, in order of first
       mention, ids pan_01, pan_02, ... One entry per size: "two 20cm sandwich tins" is ONE entry with
       "count": 2. "raw" is the recipe's words, copied exactly.
     - "shape": one of round, square, rect, loaf, ring, flat-round, sheet. "family": one of round-cake, square, traybake, sheet, loaf, ring, tart, pizza, stovetop.
     - "family" stovetop: a frying pan, skillet, sauté pan, wok, paella pan, omelette or pancake pan,
       griddle, saucepan, pot or lidded casserole pot such as a Dutch oven, whether the recipe uses it
       on the hob or in the oven; a baking dish, even one called a casserole dish, is not stovetop.
     - A baking, ovenproof, gratin, casserole or pie dish takes the family of its shape: rectangular
       traybake, square square, round round-cake, a pie dish tart. When the recipe offers a choice
       ("8-inch square or round baking dish", "8x12 or 9x13-inch dish"), use the first size and shape it names.
       A rectangle is traybake, never square: square only when its sides are equal (a 9 x 13-inch dish or
       pan is traybake).
     - "dims_mm": inside size in MILLIMETRES (inches x 25.4): "d" for a round, "l" and "w" for a
       rectangle, square or loaf; "depth" only when the recipe states it. Never guess a size.
     - "capacity_ml" only when the recipe names the tin by volume ("a 1.5 litre tin", "a 10-cup Bundt").
     - "catalog_hint" only for a tin the recipe names WITHOUT a size, from this list, else null:
       uk-loaf-1lb, uk-loaf-2lb, us-bundt-6, us-bundt-10, us-bundt-12, us-tube-10, us-jellyroll, us-quarter-sheet, us-half-sheet, us-rect-13x9, uk-traybake-30x23, uk-swissroll-33x23, eu-blech-40x30, eu-gugelhupf-22, au-lamington-30x20, au-slab-30x20, au-bar-25x8.
     - A tin with no size and none of those names ("a cake tin", "a baking dish") is not listed.
     - In the directions, wrap the words naming each listed tin in a tin token {{pan_01|words}},
       copying the words exactly: "Grease two 20cm sandwich tins" -> "Grease {{pan_01|two 20cm sandwich tins}}".
       Wrap the first mention in each step only.
     - "bake_type": one of sponge, butter-cake, quick-bread, bar, bread-lean, bread-enriched, cheesecake, custard, pizza, pie-fruit, pie-custard, pie-nut, pie-savoury, or null. Decide by how the item is made, not by its name, and
       take the first that fits, in this order:
       1. pizza: a yeast-dough base baked flat under its toppings.
       2. cheesecake: a filling that is mostly soft cheese (cream cheese, ricotta, quark) set with eggs.
       3. a pie or tart, never a recipe for pastry or dough alone: a pastry or crumb crust holds the
          filling, or a lid of pastry, a suet crust or mashed potato covers it. A sweet filling is never
          pie-savoury, whatever the crust (hot water, suet or shortcrust).
          pie-custard: the filling is mostly egg with milk or cream and sets as it bakes (custard tart,
          quiche, pumpkin and sweet potato pie, lemon meringue). pie-nut: a sugar-syrup, nut or frangipane
          filling (pecan pie, treacle tart, butter tart, Bakewell tart). pie-fruit: a sweet fruit filling
          (apple pie, mince pies, tarte tatin, sweet fruit galettes), sweet suet puddings and fruit-filled
          pastries (Sussex pond pudding, Eccles cakes, black bun), and fruit baked under a crumble, crisp or
          cobbler topping with no crust. pie-savoury: a savoury filling in or under pastry, a suet crust or
          mashed potato (meat and pot pies, pasties, empanadas, sausage rolls, beef Wellington, steak and
          kidney pudding, savoury tarts and galettes), and fish or meat under a mashed-potato lid (shepherd's,
          cottage and fish pie): a mashed-potato lid is not a topping. Never pie-savoury: a dish topped with cheese, sauce or breadcrumbs (lasagne,
          moussaka, enchiladas, gratins, aubergine parmigiana), or anything sweet.
       4. custard: an egg-set liquid baked with no crust (baked custard, crème caramel, crème brûlée,
          bread and butter pudding, clafoutis, baked rice pudding).
       5. bread-enriched / bread-lean: risen with yeast or a sourdough starter. bread-enriched when the
          dough has egg, or more than a spoonful of butter, oil, sugar or milk (brioche, challah, buns,
          rolls, babka, stollen, yeast cakes); otherwise bread-lean (baguette, sourdough, ciabatta, pitta).
       6. quick-bread: a bread, loaf, muffin or scone raised with baking powder or soda (banana bread,
          muffins, scones, soda bread, cornbread), and popovers, Yorkshire puddings and toad in the hole.
       7. butter-cake: a cake raised with baking powder or soda, its fat creamed, melted or oil (layer,
          pound, Bundt, loaf, fruit, carrot, gingerbread and upside-down cakes, cupcakes, steamed sponge
          puddings). A creamed cake
          is butter-cake even when it is called a sponge (Victoria sponge).
       8. sponge: a cake risen by eggs whisked to a foam, with little or no fat (génoise, Swiss roll,
          fatless sponge, angel food, chiffon).
       9. bar: a batter or dough baked in one pan and cut into pieces (brownies, blondies, flapjacks,
          shortbread slices).
       null: anything else, including roast meat, casseroles, cookies baked as separate pieces,
       meringues and pavlova, granola, crackers, and anything cooked on the hob.
     - "bake_step": the 0-based position in tokenized_directions (the first step is 0, not 1) of the
       step that bakes the main item, or null.
  
  SOURCE HOST: none
  
  RAW RECIPE:
  name='Placeholder Cake' photo_filename='cake.jpg' servings='8' prep_time='20 mins' cook_time='35 mins' ingredients=['2 cups/250g plain flour', '1 cup sugar'] directions=['Mix everything.', 'Bake until done.'] notes=None categories=['EPUB Imports']
  
  '''
# ---
# name: TestPromptSnapshots.test_refine_prompt_with_a_host
  '''
  
  You are a culinary data refiner. Transform this raw recipe into the structured Cayenne format.
  
  RULES:
  1. STRUCTURED INGREDIENTS:
     - Assign each entry a unique ID (ing_01, ing_02, etc.).
     - Extract numeric "amount", "unit" (null if unitless), and "name".
     - "fallback_string" is the original full line, unchanged.
     - Every line of the ingredients list gets exactly one entry, in the order the lines appear,
       and "line_index" is that line's 0-based position in the RAW RECIPE ingredients list.
     - "kind": "heading" for a line that names a part of the recipe and lists nothing to buy:
       "For the sauce:", "PUDDING", "To serve:", "Garnish", a bold phase such as "**Phase 1**".
       "kind": "ingredient" for every other line, including a food with no amount
       ("Butter for greasing", "Salt to taste", "To serve: crusty bread").
     - A heading has "name" set to its words without emphasis or a trailing colon, "fallback_string"
       the line unchanged, and "amount", "unit", "converted_amount", "converted_unit", "state" null.
       Number headings in the same ing_01, ing_02 sequence as ingredients.
     - STATE: for every ingredient with an amount and a volume or weight unit, say whether it is
       "liquid" - pourable as the recipe uses it: water, milk, cream, stock, oil, wine, juice,
       vinegar, honey, syrup, melted butter - or "solid" - everything else, including flour,
       sugar, salt, cold butter, chopped vegetables and meat. Put it in "state". Leave "state"
       null when the line has no amount or its unit is neither a volume nor a weight.
       Use the state when you compute the conversion below.
     - DUAL MEASURES: when a line states two measures ("1 cup (240 g) flour", "250g/2 cups flour",
       "1 ounce (about 1/3 packed cup) grated pecorino"), the FIRST measure the writer gives is
       "amount" and "unit". When the second is the other kind (a weight beside a volume, or a volume
       beside a weight), put it in "converted_amount" and "converted_unit" exactly as the line writes
       it and set "is_ai_converted" to false. When both are the same kind ("1 pound (450g)"), the
       second is not structured: apply the CONVERSION rule below.
     - CONVERSION: for a line with one measure, give the equivalent in the OTHER measure, in grams or
       millilitres only: a weight for a volume ("1 cup" flour -> 120, "g"), a volume for a weight
       ("150 g" flour -> 300, "ml"). Never answer in cups, spoons, ounces or pints. Put it in
       "converted_amount" and "converted_unit" and set "is_ai_converted" to true. Leave
       "converted_amount" and "converted_unit" null and "is_ai_converted" false when the line
       states no amount ("salt to taste") or its unit is neither a volume nor a weight ("3 eggs",
       "a pinch").
       Sizes for older British measures: gill = 142 ml (a US gill = 118 ml); teacup = 142 ml; breakfast cup = 227 ml; dessertspoon = 10 ml; stone = 14 lb; dram = 1/16 oz (a weight).
     - Never change a number or a unit the line writes: "amount" and "unit" are the line's own.
     - amount: the numeric quantity. Use null - never 0 - when the source states no
       amount ("salt to taste", "a pinch of nutmeg"). A zero would be read as a real
       measurement of nothing.
  
  2. TOKENIZED DIRECTIONS:
     - Rewrite each direction with Fat Tokens: {{ingredient_id|words|use}}. Copy the direction
       exactly; replacing every token with its words must give back the direction as written.
     - Say how much of the ingredient each mention uses, as "use":
         * all  - the whole amount the ingredient line gives goes in here, at once. Only the FIRST
           time it does: later mentions of the same food ("drain the rice", "until the rice is
           done", "the butter mixture") are none.
         * an amount - the direction itself writes a quantity for this mention. The words are ONLY
           that quantity as written ("1/2 cup (60 g)", "2", "two tablespoons"), never the
           ingredient's name ("Add 1 cup peas": the words are "1 cup", not "1 cup peas"), and the
           use is the quantity as a decimal number and a unit: "0.5 cup", "60 g", "2" for a count.
           If the direction names the ingredient without writing a quantity beside it, the use is
           all, rest or none - never an amount, even when you know the line's amount.
         * rest - what is left after amounts the directions stated earlier: "the remaining flour",
           "the rest of the sugar". The words are the ingredient's name.
         * none - the ingredient is named but no amount follows from the text: "add more flour
           until sticky", "a bit more", "season with salt", "salt to taste", "Salt it", a share
           ("half the cider", "a third of the flour"), a rate ("1 teaspoon at a time", "2 per
           ball"), or a later mention of food already added.
     - When unsure, say "none". A missing number is safe; a wrong one is not.
     - Wrap only the ingredient's words, never "the", "your" or "of": "the flour" -> the words are "flour".
     - Examples:
         "Whisk together the flour and salt" -> "Whisk together the {{ing_01|flour|all}} and {{ing_03|salt|all}}"
         "Add about 1/2 cup (60 g) flour" -> "Add about {{ing_02|1/2 cup (60 g)|0.5 cup}} flour"
         "Add the remaining flour" -> "Add the remaining {{ing_02|flour|rest}}"
         "Add more flour until sticky" -> "Add more {{ing_02|flour|none}} until sticky"
     - TEMPERATURES: wrap every temperature a direction states - an oven, a grill, frying oil, sugar,
       water, proving, a core temperature ("until the centre reads 63°C"), a fridge or a freezer - in a
       temperature token {{temp:VALUE|words}}. The words are the direction's own, copied exactly,
       and the whole run when it gives one setting several ways: "180°C/160°C fan/gas mark 4" is ONE
       token. VALUE is what the writer meant, in the writer's own unit:
         * the figure: a number and C or F ("180C", "350F"), or a Gas Mark ("gas4", "gas0.5", "gas0.25");
         * then ",oven" when it is an oven setting (bake, roast, preheat, a described oven);
         * then ",fan=160C" (or F) when the writer also gives the fan-oven figure;
         * "fan=160C" alone when the writer gives only a fan-oven figure.
         * ",core" (and nothing else after the figure) when it is the food's internal temperature,
           the doneness check: "until the centre reads 200°F", "an internal temperature of 93°C".
       A number with no unit ("bake at 180") or a described oven: decide from the recipe and its
       SOURCE SYSTEM; a described oven is slow 150C, moderate 180C, hot 220C, very hot 230C, each
       with ",oven". Never wrap a range ("170-180°C") or words with no figure ("room temperature",
       "warm", "lukewarm").
     - Examples:
         "Preheat the oven to 350°F" -> "Preheat the oven to {{temp:350F,oven|350°F}}"
         "Bake at 180°C/160°C fan/gas mark 4" -> "Bake at {{temp:180C,oven,fan=160C|180°C/160°C fan/gas mark 4}}"
         "Heat the oil to 180C" -> "Heat the oil to {{temp:180C|180C}}"
         "Bake in a moderate oven" -> "Bake in a {{temp:180C,oven|moderate oven}}"
         "Bake until it reads 93°C in the centre" -> "Bake until it reads {{temp:93C,core|93°C}} in the centre"
  
  3. PHASES:
     - If the raw recipe groups its ingredients or directions into phases,
       stages, or days (a bold heading entry such as "**Phase 1**", "**Day 1**",
       "**Soaker**"), keep every one of those headings as its own entry, in
       place, in both lists.
     - A heading is not an ingredient: in the ingredients list it is an entry with "kind": "heading"
       (rule 1); in the directions it is a step whose text is the heading, with no fat tokens.
       Never put a heading's id in a fat token.
     - Do NOT flatten, merge, renumber, or drop a phase. The reader must still
       be able to tell where one session ends and the next begins.
  
  4. SOURCE SYSTEM:
     - "source_uom_system_detected": the measuring system the writer used, exactly one of "US",
       "UK", "EU", "AU", "Imperial", judged only from evidence in the RAW RECIPE or the SOURCE HOST:
         * a size the recipe states for a unit: "1 cup (240 ml)" or "(237 ml)" is US; "1 cup (250 ml)"
           is a metric cup (UK, EU or AU); "1 tbsp (20 ml)" is AU; "1 pint (568 ml)" or a 20 fl oz
           pint is Imperial;
         * the pre-metric British measures: "breakfast cup", "teacup" or "stone" as a measure, or "gill" when nothing points to the US, is Imperial; "dessertspoon" alone is not evidence (modern UK and Australian recipes still use it);
         * a statement about the measures ("Australian standard measures", "US cup measures");
         * the SOURCE HOST: .co.uk is UK, .com.au is AU, .co.nz is UK (New Zealand's measures are UK
           Metric's);
         * spelling and ingredient names only one country uses ("caster sugar", "plain flour" and
           "double cream" are UK; "all-purpose flour" and "heavy cream" are US).
       Grams printed beside cups are not evidence: American writers print them too.
     - "source_uom_system_evidence": the words you relied on, quoted exactly from the RAW RECIPE, or
       the SOURCE HOST exactly as given. One short quote.
     - Leave both null when nothing above applies.
  
  5. TINS AND BAKING:
     - "pans": every baking tin, pan, tray or dish the recipe names with a size, in order of first
       mention, ids pan_01, pan_02, ... One entry per size: "two 20cm sandwich tins" is ONE entry with
       "count": 2. "raw" is the recipe's words, copied exactly.
     - "shape": one of round, square, rect, loaf, ring, flat-round, sheet. "family": one of round-cake, square, traybake, sheet, loaf, ring, tart, pizza, stovetop.
     - "family" stovetop: a frying pan, skillet, sauté pan, wok, paella pan, omelette or pancake pan,
       griddle, saucepan, pot or lidded casserole pot such as a Dutch oven, whether the recipe uses it
       on the hob or in the oven; a baking dish, even one called a casserole dish, is not stovetop.
     - A baking, ovenproof, gratin, casserole or pie dish takes the family of its shape: rectangular
       traybake, square square, round round-cake, a pie dish tart. When the recipe offers a choice
       ("8-inch square or round baking dish", "8x12 or 9x13-inch dish"), use the first size and shape it names.
       A rectangle is traybake, never square: square only when its sides are equal (a 9 x 13-inch dish or
       pan is traybake).
     - "dims_mm": inside size in MILLIMETRES (inches x 25.4): "d" for a round, "l" and "w" for a
       rectangle, square or loaf; "depth" only when the recipe states it. Never guess a size.
     - "capacity_ml" only when the recipe names the tin by volume ("a 1.5 litre tin", "a 10-cup Bundt").
     - "catalog_hint" only for a tin the recipe names WITHOUT a size, from this list, else null:
       uk-loaf-1lb, uk-loaf-2lb, us-bundt-6, us-bundt-10, us-bundt-12, us-tube-10, us-jellyroll, us-quarter-sheet, us-half-sheet, us-rect-13x9, uk-traybake-30x23, uk-swissroll-33x23, eu-blech-40x30, eu-gugelhupf-22, au-lamington-30x20, au-slab-30x20, au-bar-25x8.
     - A tin with no size and none of those names ("a cake tin", "a baking dish") is not listed.
     - In the directions, wrap the words naming each listed tin in a tin token {{pan_01|words}},
       copying the words exactly: "Grease two 20cm sandwich tins" -> "Grease {{pan_01|two 20cm sandwich tins}}".
       Wrap the first mention in each step only.
     - "bake_type": one of sponge, butter-cake, quick-bread, bar, bread-lean, bread-enriched, cheesecake, custard, pizza, pie-fruit, pie-custard, pie-nut, pie-savoury, or null. Decide by how the item is made, not by its name, and
       take the first that fits, in this order:
       1. pizza: a yeast-dough base baked flat under its toppings.
       2. cheesecake: a filling that is mostly soft cheese (cream cheese, ricotta, quark) set with eggs.
       3. a pie or tart, never a recipe for pastry or dough alone: a pastry or crumb crust holds the
          filling, or a lid of pastry, a suet crust or mashed potato covers it. A sweet filling is never
          pie-savoury, whatever the crust (hot water, suet or shortcrust).
          pie-custard: the filling is mostly egg with milk or cream and sets as it bakes (custard tart,
          quiche, pumpkin and sweet potato pie, lemon meringue). pie-nut: a sugar-syrup, nut or frangipane
          filling (pecan pie, treacle tart, butter tart, Bakewell tart). pie-fruit: a sweet fruit filling
          (apple pie, mince pies, tarte tatin, sweet fruit galettes), sweet suet puddings and fruit-filled
          pastries (Sussex pond pudding, Eccles cakes, black bun), and fruit baked under a crumble, crisp or
          cobbler topping with no crust. pie-savoury: a savoury filling in or under pastry, a suet crust or
          mashed potato (meat and pot pies, pasties, empanadas, sausage rolls, beef Wellington, steak and
          kidney pudding, savoury tarts and galettes), and fish or meat under a mashed-potato lid (shepherd's,
          cottage and fish pie): a mashed-potato lid is not a topping. Never pie-savoury: a dish topped with cheese, sauce or breadcrumbs (lasagne,
          moussaka, enchiladas, gratins, aubergine parmigiana), or anything sweet.
       4. custard: an egg-set liquid baked with no crust (baked custard, crème caramel, crème brûlée,
          bread and butter pudding, clafoutis, baked rice pudding).
       5. bread-enriched / bread-lean: risen with yeast or a sourdough starter. bread-enriched when the
          dough has egg, or more than a spoonful of butter, oil, sugar or milk (brioche, challah, buns,
          rolls, babka, stollen, yeast cakes); otherwise bread-lean (baguette, sourdough, ciabatta, pitta).
       6. quick-bread: a bread, loaf, muffin or scone raised with baking powder or soda (banana bread,
          muffins, scones, soda bread, cornbread), and popovers, Yorkshire puddings and toad in the hole.
       7. butter-cake: a cake raised with baking powder or soda, its fat creamed, melted or oil (layer,
          pound, Bundt, loaf, fruit, carrot, gingerbread and upside-down cakes, cupcakes, steamed sponge
          puddings). A creamed cake
          is butter-cake even when it is called a sponge (Victoria sponge).
       8. sponge: a cake risen by eggs whisked to a foam, with little or no fat (génoise, Swiss roll,
          fatless sponge, angel food, chiffon).
       9. bar: a batter or dough baked in one pan and cut into pieces (brownies, blondies, flapjacks,
          shortbread slices).
       null: anything else, including roast meat, casseroles, cookies baked as separate pieces,
       meringues and pavlova, granola, crackers, and anything cooked on the hob.
     - "bake_step": the 0-based position in tokenized_directions (the first step is 0, not 1) of the
       step that bakes the main item, or null.
  
  SOURCE HOST: taste.com.au
  
  RAW RECIPE:
  name='Placeholder Cake' photo_filename='cake.jpg' servings='8' prep_time='20 mins' cook_time='35 mins' ingredients=['2 cups/250g plain flour', '1 cup sugar'] directions=['Mix everything.', 'Bake until done.'] notes=None categories=['EPUB Imports']
  
  '''
# ---
# name: TestPromptSnapshots.test_table_prompt
  '''
  The following text is from a recipe book and contains one or more ingredient tables
  where each ingredient name, its weight, its volume measure, and its baker's percentage
  appear on separate lines rather than in columns.
  
  Reformat ONLY the ingredient table sections so that each ingredient appears on a single
  line in the format: "IngredientName: weight (volume) — baker's%"
  
  Do NOT change any recipe titles, headings, method steps, notes, or any other text.
  Do NOT add or remove any ingredients or values.
  Preserve all [IMAGE: ...] markers exactly as they appear.
  
  Text:
  PLACEHOLDER CHUNK BODY — fixed text so the snapshot only moves when the template does.
  '''
# ---
# name: TestPromptSnapshots.test_temperatures_prompt
  '''
  
  You are a culinary data refiner. Find every temperature these recipe directions state that is not
  already written with its unit, and say what the writer meant.
  
  RULES:
     - Report every temperature figure with no C or F after it ("bake at 180", "350 degrees", "375°",
       "a 400° oven", "preheat to 425") and every described oven ("a moderate oven", "a hot oven"). A
       degree sign alone is not a unit. Skip anything inside {{ }} and any figure already followed
       by C, F, Celsius or Fahrenheit.
     - "quote" is the words to wrap, copied character for character from that step; "context" is the
       quote with about five words around it, copied the same way.
     - "value" is what the writer meant, in the writer's unit: a number and C or F ("180C", "350F"),
       then ",oven" when it is an oven setting. Decide the unit from the SOURCE SYSTEM and the figure:
       US recipes write Fahrenheit; UK, EU and AU recipes write Celsius; an oven figure above 260 is
       Fahrenheit. A described oven is slow 150C, moderate 180C, hot 220C, very hot 230C, each with ",oven".
     - Never report a range ("170-180"), an angle ("turn it 90°"), a time, a weight, a size or a count.
     - An oven, grill or baking figure is always reported, even when the unit must be inferred.
  
  SOURCE SYSTEM: UK
  
  DIRECTIONS:
    1. Bake in a moderate oven.
    2. Serve.
  
  '''
# ---
# name: TestPromptSnapshots.test_toc_classify_prompt
  '''
  Given this list of table-of-contents entries from a cookbook, identify which ones are
  specific recipe or dish names (e.g. "Chocolate Chip Cookies", "Beef Stew", "Roast Chicken")
  vs section/chapter headers (e.g. "Soups", "Desserts", "Introduction", "Breakfast").
  
  Return JSON: {"recipe_indices": [0, 1, 3, 5, ...]} — the 0-based indices of entries that are recipe titles.
  Section headers like "Soups", "Desserts" should NOT be included. Only include entries that look like
  specific dish/recipe names.
  
  Entries (one per line):
  0. Soups
  1. Boiled Custard
  
  '''
# ---
# name: TestPromptSnapshots.test_toc_parse_prompt
  '''
  This text is from the table-of-contents or contents page of a recipe book.
  Extract the list of recipe/section titles in order. Include page numbers if they appear.
  
  Output JSON: {"entries": [{"title": "...", "page": <int or null>}, ...]}
  Use page: null when no page number is given.
  Include only substantive entries (skip "Contents", "Index", etc. if they are standalone headers).
  
  Text:
  Contents
  
  Soups .... 3
  '''
# ---
# name: TestSchemaSnapshots.test_cayenne_refinement_schema
  dict({
    '$defs': dict({
      'PanDims': dict({
        'description': "A tin's inside size in millimetres.",
        'properties': dict({
          'd': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'number',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
            'description': 'Diameter, for a round tin.',
            'title': 'D',
          }),
          'depth': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'number',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
            'description': 'Depth, only if the recipe states it.',
            'title': 'Depth',
          }),
          'l': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'number',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
            'description': 'Length, for a rectangle or loaf.',
            'title': 'L',
          }),
          'w': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'number',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
            'description': 'Width, for a rectangle or loaf.',
            'title': 'W',
          }),
        }),
        'title': 'PanDims',
        'type': 'object',
      }),
      'RecipePan': dict({
        'description': 'One kind of tin a recipe bakes in.',
        'properties': dict({
          'capacity_ml': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'number',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
            'description': "Only when the recipe names the tin by volume ('a 1.5 litre tin', 'a 10-cup Bundt').",
            'title': 'Capacity Ml',
          }),
          'catalog_hint': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'string',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
            'description': 'Only for the named tins listed in the prompt; otherwise null.',
            'title': 'Catalog Hint',
          }),
          'count': dict({
            'default': 1,
            'description': 'How many of this tin the recipe uses (two 20 cm tins → 2).',
            'title': 'Count',
            'type': 'integer',
          }),
          'dims_mm': dict({
            '$ref': '#/$defs/PanDims',
            'description': 'Inside size in millimetres (inches × 25.4): d for a round, l and w for a rectangle or loaf, depth only if the recipe states it.',
          }),
          'family': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'string',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
            'description': 'round-cake, square, traybake, sheet, loaf, ring, tart, pizza, stovetop; null if unknown.',
            'title': 'Family',
          }),
          'id': dict({
            'description': 'pan_01, pan_02, … in order of first mention.',
            'title': 'Id',
            'type': 'string',
          }),
          'raw': dict({
            'description': "The recipe's words naming the tin(s), copied exactly.",
            'title': 'Raw',
            'type': 'string',
          }),
          'shape': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'string',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
            'description': 'round, square, rect, loaf, ring, flat-round, sheet; null if unknown.',
            'title': 'Shape',
          }),
        }),
        'required': list([
          'id',
          'raw',
        ]),
        'title': 'RecipePan',
        'type': 'object',
      }),
      'StructuredIngredient': dict({
        'properties': dict({
          'amount': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'number',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
            'description': "Numeric quantity, e.g. 1.5. null when the source states no amount ('salt to taste').",
            'title': 'Amount',
          }),
          'converted_amount': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'number',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
            'description': "The OTHER measure's amount: the writer's own second measure, or the AI's in grams or millilitres.",
            'title': 'Converted Amount',
          }),
          'converted_unit': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'string',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
            'description': 'The OTHER measure\'s unit: the writer\'s own, else "g" or "ml".',
            'title': 'Converted Unit',
          }),
          'fallback_string': dict({
            'description': 'Full original string, e.g., "1 1/2 cups flour".',
            'title': 'Fallback String',
            'type': 'string',
          }),
          'id': dict({
            'description': 'Unique ID for cross-linking, e.g., "ing_01"',
            'title': 'Id',
            'type': 'string',
          }),
          'is_ai_converted': dict({
            'default': False,
            'description': "True if the AI calculated the conversion; false for the writer's own second measure.",
            'title': 'Is Ai Converted',
            'type': 'boolean',
          }),
          'kind': dict({
            'description': '"heading" for a line that names a part of the recipe and lists nothing to buy ("For the sauce:", "PUDDING", "To serve:"); "ingredient" for every other line, including a food with no amount ("Butter for greasing").',
            'enum': list([
              'ingredient',
              'heading',
            ]),
            'title': 'Kind',
            'type': 'string',
          }),
          'line_index': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'integer',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
            'description': '0-based index of the raw ingredient line this entry was parsed from. Every line, heading or ingredient, yields exactly one entry. null only when the entry is not tied to a single line.',
            'title': 'Line Index',
          }),
          'name': dict({
            'description': 'Core name, e.g., "flour".',
            'title': 'Name',
            'type': 'string',
          }),
          'state': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'enum': list([
                  'liquid',
                  'solid',
                ]),
                'type': 'string',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
            'description': 'Whether the ingredient is pourable as the recipe uses it (liquid) or not (solid). Stated before the conversion is computed, because the conversion depends on it (cookbook locales D13). null when the line has no amount or no volume/weight unit.',
            'title': 'State',
          }),
          'unit': dict({
            'anyOf': list([
              dict({
                'type': 'string',
              }),
              dict({
                'type': 'null',
              }),
            ]),
            'default': None,
            'description': 'Unit of measure, e.g., "cups".',
            'title': 'Unit',
          }),
        }),
        'required': list([
          'id',
          'kind',
          'name',
          'fallback_string',
        ]),
        'title': 'StructuredIngredient',
        'type': 'object',
      }),
      'TokenizedDirection': dict({
        'properties': dict({
          'step': dict({
            'description': '1-based step number.',
            'title': 'Step',
            'type': 'integer',
          }),
          'text': dict({
            'description': "Direction text with Fat Tokens {{id|words|use}}: words exactly as written, use one of all, rest, none, or an amount such as '0.5 cup'; and temperature tokens {{temp:VALUE|words}}, VALUE such as '180C,oven,fan=160C', '350F' or 'gas4'.",
            'title': 'Text',
            'type': 'string',
          }),
        }),
        'required': list([
          'step',
          'text',
        ]),
        'title': 'TokenizedDirection',
        'type': 'object',
      }),
    }),
    'description': 'Internal refinement pass output.',
    'properties': dict({
      'bake_step': dict({
        'anyOf': list([
          dict({
            'type': 'integer',
          }),
          dict({
            'type': 'null',
          }),
        ]),
        'default': None,
        'description': '0-based index into tokenized_directions of the step that bakes; null when nothing bakes.',
        'title': 'Bake Step',
      }),
      'bake_type': dict({
        'anyOf': list([
          dict({
            'type': 'string',
          }),
          dict({
            'type': 'null',
          }),
        ]),
        'default': None,
        'description': "What the recipe bakes, one word from the prompt's list of 13 bake types; null when nothing bakes.",
        'title': 'Bake Type',
      }),
      'base_servings': dict({
        'anyOf': list([
          dict({
            'type': 'integer',
          }),
          dict({
            'type': 'null',
          }),
        ]),
        'title': 'Base Servings',
      }),
      'pans': dict({
        'description': 'The tins the recipe bakes in; [] when none.',
        'items': dict({
          '$ref': '#/$defs/RecipePan',
        }),
        'title': 'Pans',
        'type': 'array',
      }),
      'source_uom_system_detected': dict({
        'anyOf': list([
          dict({
            'type': 'string',
          }),
          dict({
            'type': 'null',
          }),
        ]),
        'default': None,
        'description': 'The measuring system the writer used: one of "US", "UK", "EU", "AU", "Imperial", judged only from evidence in the recipe or its source host. null when there is no evidence.',
        'title': 'Source Uom System Detected',
      }),
      'source_uom_system_evidence': dict({
        'anyOf': list([
          dict({
            'type': 'string',
          }),
          dict({
            'type': 'null',
          }),
        ]),
        'default': None,
        'description': 'The words that judgement rests on, quoted exactly from the recipe, or the source host exactly as given.',
        'title': 'Source Uom System Evidence',
      }),
      'structured_ingredients': dict({
        'items': dict({
          '$ref': '#/$defs/StructuredIngredient',
        }),
        'title': 'Structured Ingredients',
        'type': 'array',
      }),
      'title': dict({
        'title': 'Title',
        'type': 'string',
      }),
      'tokenized_directions': dict({
        'items': dict({
          '$ref': '#/$defs/TokenizedDirection',
        }),
        'title': 'Tokenized Directions',
        'type': 'array',
      }),
    }),
    'required': list([
      'title',
      'base_servings',
      'structured_ingredients',
      'tokenized_directions',
    ]),
    'title': 'CayenneRefinement',
    'type': 'object',
  })
